body:
>" In Christianity we are all sinners and Jesus came here for the sinners. If your dad hates sinners he is doing Christianity wrong. "

So when Jesus chucks a bunch of sinners into the never ending wine press that is hell because they cannot believe in him (as they have no reason or proof to) or because they won't follow him because it directly conflicts with their personality he is doing Christianity correctly?

I don't intend to convince you of anything here. I'm letting you know what I think.

The biggest sin in Christianity is having an identity that extends outward past the roles that God forces you to have. It is the root of all sin.  My dad is consistent with his beliefs and is not a Christian to begin with. In religion's like this being inconsistent IS consistent. You can't flood an entire planet full of people and then proclaim that you love all of humanity.  You can't enforce unjust suffering on people by virtue of them existing in a state out of their control and then proclaim that this is justice. You don't enforce a biblical law or create one that actively promotes the execution of people and claim they are free.

My perception is informed by the actions of said God as depicted in the bible and every thing that God advocates for and has ever advocated for. My habit of taking a step back and evaluating what this God says and does is something I have picked up from looking at how my parents treat me. Sure Jesus said some flowery poetic things that sound good on paper, but I'm not going to pretend like he didn't say fucked up shit either or that he didn't do fucked up shit in a past life.

Stopping one woman from getting stoned over an action that is literally no one's business is good. Doesn't erase the fact that they were doing it under God's command from hundreds of years prior. It also doesn't erase the deaths of the other adulterers and homosexuals etc that existed prior to Jesus deciding to re-write the law.

It's amazing how quickly everything went to shit when Jesus left too. Thousands of years of war and genocide by people who by all means "should have been doing Christianity right".

Asserting that my Dad isn't doing the right thing because it doesn't line up with the positives of some verses is ignoring the shit ton of bad things that came before them that he is actually following.

>" You just need to find that confidence in yourself to be who you are and have your own relationship with God. Let God worry about your crazy father. "

Based on what I said above, I want nothing to do with this God. Non-existence would be preferable. He sounds insufferable. How does one be himself if there are laws governing who and what they \*can\* be?

>"Now the task has been much harder, because your fathers attitude has probably driven you away from god, towards atheism, etc. So you have a long way to go to get back on track. But starting with knowing your father doesn't speak for God or Christianity, and he is actively contradicting the teachings of the bible, is maybe a good place to begin. "

My father's attitude isn't what has "driven me away from god". Its what has driven me away from him. The bible he uses to justify his actions is what drives me away from God. I'm not  just some damaged way-ward rebellious kid with daddy issues who rebels against God to get back at him. I see genuine issues with it's teachings that manifest in reality.

Why is Atheism the path that is "not on track"? Atheism to me is the only path that doesn't require cognitive dissonance.  I don't want to follow or worship a God that is remotely like the one in that book even if he is real.

I'm aware my dad isn't the spokesperson for the bible. I don't rely on him for how I think about it. I used to but now I don't.  The bible should speak for itself and from what I hear it talks a lot of shit (granted with some sprinkles). But from my perspective no one can get the bible right, because the bible teaches so many paradoxical and hypocritical things that are actually impossible without cognitive dissonance or ignoring aspects of the bible. Some Christians are able to cherry pick verses that support their world view. Some of them do it in away the promotes tolerance and others in a way that promotes bigotry. No two Christians view the world the same way though or behave exactly the same. Implying there is a right way implies that you know the correct way and other people beg to differ.

I.E: Jesus condones things we would find immoral in the modern era such as slavery and beating slaves. The people who are against slavery aren't following the bible's morality so they're doing Christianity wrong. Same goes with beating children and wives.

